# Expectation record for 09_optimization_levels/002_loop_invariance.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 002_loop_invariance
area               = 09_optimization_levels
description        = Loop results byte-identical at -O0, -O1 and -O2 under whatever transformations each compiler's pipeline applies -- shapes chosen so that invariant motion and strength reduction are genuinely applicable, with no pass and no surviving loop assumed
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour. No signed overflow anywhere: every loop bound
is at most 1000 and every accumulator stays far inside int range, the largest being
sum_big at 499500, which is about 0.02 percent of INT_MAX. Every induction variable is
bounded by 1000 and is assigned before every use, so there is no read of uninitialized
storage. The only division is i % 3, whose divisor is non-zero and whose dividend is
non-negative. No shift operator appears. No pointer arithmetic, no aliasing violation, no
object modified twice between sequence points, at most one side-effecting argument per
call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on relative addresses.
Several volatile objects are read within a single expression; their relative order is
unspecified, but no volatile object is modified anywhere in the program, so every read of
a given object yields the same value and the printed output does not depend on the
ordering.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No implementation-defined behaviour is observed. Widths are normalized: only int is used,
which is 32 bits on all four targets, and long is never used because sizeof(long) is 4 on
i686 and 8 on the other three targets. Plain char is not used, so the measured plain-char
signedness difference (signed on x86-64 and i686, unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64)
cannot be observed. No address, pointer value, sizeof or _Alignof result is printed. No
floating point is used.
WHAT THIS PROGRAM ASSERTS, STATED EXACTLY, because a wider claim would be unverifiable from its
output. It asserts that the eight printed results are identical at -O0, -O1 and -O2 on every
target, and it assumes nothing about which transformations produce that. It does NOT assert that
any particular transformation ran: nothing in a program's stdout can reveal which passes fired,
and the two compilers under comparison document different optimization sets, which is the very
difference that must not be observable. The documented pipeline for the compiler under test is
-O0 with no passes, -O1 with mem2reg, constant folding and dead-code elimination, and -O2 adding
common-subexpression elimination and simplification iterated to a fixed point
(docs/technical-specifications.md line 535). Loop-invariant code motion and strength reduction
are not among them, and loop unrolling and vectorization are explicitly out of scope for this
project, so this record neither names nor requires any of the four. The reference compiler
applies a larger and differently documented set, and that asymmetry is precisely why only the
printed values may be compared.
THE LOOP SHAPES ARE STILL CHOSEN SO THAT THE TWO TRANSFORMATIONS THE PLAN NAMES FOR THIS PROGRAM
ARE GENUINELY APPLICABLE -- sum_inv adds the loop-invariant product k * m, and sum_mul and
sum_pow2 accumulate i * k and i * 8, the textbook strength-reduction candidates -- so the
invariance being asserted is invariance across those transformations being applied or not
applied, by either compiler, at any level. Loop unrolling is deliberately NOT named as covered:
the repository documents no unroller for the compiler under test and lists loop unrolling among
the transformations explicitly out of scope (docs/technical-specifications.md line 717), and the
minimal shared flag set gives no guarantee
that the reference compiler unrolls either, so a claim of unrolling coverage would be a claim
this program cannot support.
MOST BOUNDS AND FACTORS ARE READ FROM VOLATILE STORAGE, which means those loops cannot be reduced
to a constant by the folder alone and the backend must emit real loop code -- that is what keeps
the runtime path under test at -O1 and -O2. One loop is a deliberate exception and is named
rather than left implicit: the do-while whose controlling expression is the literal-false i < 0
runs exactly once by construction, which is the single-iteration boundary it exists to check, and
it may legitimately be folded straight to its result of 5. No loop anywhere in this program is
required to survive translation, and no line below depends on generated code: a loop that is
unrolled, rotated, inverted, turned into a closed form or eliminated entirely still produces the
value that is compared.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
sum_mul=135
sum_inv=210
sum_pow2=360
sum_big=499500
down=55
once=5
nested=1540
guarded=147
END
